__d(function(e,t,i,o,a,r,n){a.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Textbook for Class VII",chapter_number:2,chapter_title:"Fractions and Decimals",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How do you convert a mixed number to an improper fraction? Convert 3\u2154.",back:"Formula: Mixed number a(b/c) = (a\xd7c + b)/c. For 3\u2154: (3\xd73 + 2)/3 = (9+2)/3 = 11/3. Always multiply the whole number by the denominator, add the numerator, and keep the same denominator. Reverse: 11\xf73 = 3 remainder 2 \u2192 3\u2154.",memory_hook:"Mixed to Improper: (Whole \xd7 Bottom + Top) / Bottom. 'Multiply and Add on top, Bottom stays'. 3\u2154 \u2192 3\xd73=9, 9+2=11, put over 3 \u2192 11/3."},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the reciprocal of a fraction? Find the reciprocal of: (a) 3/7 (b) 5 (c) 1/9",back:"The reciprocal of a fraction a/b is b/a \u2014 you flip the numerator and denominator. The product of a number and its reciprocal is always 1. (a) Reciprocal of 3/7 = 7/3. Check: 3/7 \xd7 7/3 = 21/21 = 1 \u2713. (b) Reciprocal of 5 = 1/5. Check: 5 \xd7 1/5 = 1 \u2713. (c) Reciprocal of 1/9 = 9/1 = 9. Check: 1/9 \xd7 9 = 1 \u2713. Note: 0 has no reciprocal.",memory_hook:"Reciprocal = FLIP. Top becomes bottom, bottom becomes top. Always gives 1 when multiplied. 'Every number has an equal and opposite reciprocal that cancels it to 1.'"},{id:"fc03",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does 'of' mean in mathematics? Find \u2154 of 27.",back:"'Of' in mathematics means multiplication (\xd7). So 'fraction of a number' = fraction \xd7 number. \u2154 of 27 = \u2154 \xd7 27 = (2\xd727)/3 = 54/3 = 18. Real-life: '\xbe of the class passed' = \xbe \xd7 (class size). '\xbd of 50 kg' = 25 kg.",memory_hook:"'Of' = multiply (\xd7). Always. \xbe of 40 = \xbe \xd7 40 = 30. This is the most important shortcut for fraction word problems. See 'of' \u2192 write '\xd7'."},{id:"fc04",type:"Computation",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How do you multiply two fractions? Find: (4/5) \xd7 (3/8)",back:"Multiply numerators together and denominators together: (a/b) \xd7 (c/d) = (a\xd7c)/(b\xd7d). (4/5) \xd7 (3/8) = (4\xd73)/(5\xd78) = 12/40 = 3/10. Tip: Cancel common factors BEFORE multiplying to simplify. Here: 4 and 8 share factor 4 \u2192 1/5 \xd7 3/2 = 3/10 directly.",memory_hook:"Fraction multiplication: TOP \xd7 TOP / BOTTOM \xd7 BOTTOM. Cancel first if possible \u2014 makes the numbers smaller. Like simplifying BEFORE the big calculation saves effort."},{id:"fc05",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"State the KCF rule for dividing fractions. Use it to find: (5/6) \xf7 (2/3)",back:"KCF = Keep, Change, Flip. Keep the first fraction as is. Change the \xf7 sign to \xd7. Flip (take reciprocal of) the second fraction. (5/6) \xf7 (2/3) \u2192 Keep (5/6), Change to \xd7, Flip (2/3 \u2192 3/2) \u2192 (5/6) \xd7 (3/2) = 15/12 = 5/4 = 1\xbc.",memory_hook:"KCF: Keep the first, Change \xf7 to \xd7, Flip the second. Only the SECOND fraction is flipped \u2014 never the first. Like a cooking recipe: Keep the dish, Change the method, Flip the pan."},{id:"fc06",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A piece of rope is 7\xbd metres long. It is cut into pieces of \xbe metre each. How many pieces are made?",back:"Number of pieces = Total length \xf7 Length of each piece = 7\xbd \xf7 \xbe. Convert: 7\xbd = 15/2. Apply KCF: (15/2) \xf7 (3/4) = (15/2) \xd7 (4/3) = 60/6 = 10. Answer: 10 pieces of \xbe metre each.",memory_hook:"Cutting/sharing problems always involve DIVISION of fractions. 'How many pieces of size X can you cut from Y?' = Y \xf7 X. Always convert mixed numbers first."},{id:"fc07",type:"Computation",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Find 2\u2153 \xd7 1\u2158",back:"Step 1: Convert to improper fractions. 2\u2153 = (2\xd73+1)/3 = 7/3. 1\u2158 = (1\xd75+4)/5 = 9/5. Step 2: Multiply. (7/3) \xd7 (9/5) = 63/15 = 21/5. Step 3: Convert. 21\xf75 = 4 remainder 1 \u2192 4\u2155. Answer: 4\u2155.",memory_hook:"Mixed \xd7 Mixed: Convert BOTH first, then multiply. Never multiply mixed numbers directly without converting \u2014 it leads to errors. Convert \u2192 Multiply \u2192 Simplify \u2192 Convert back."},{id:"fc08",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How many decimal places does the product of 1.23 \xd7 0.05 have? Find the product.",back:"Count decimal places: 1.23 has 2 decimal places. 0.05 has 2 decimal places. Total = 4 decimal places in the product. Multiply as whole numbers: 123 \xd7 5 = 615. Insert 4 decimal places: 0.0615. Answer: 1.23 \xd7 0.05 = 0.0615. Always add zeros to the left if needed.",memory_hook:"Decimal multiplication: ADD the decimal place counts. 2 + 2 = 4 places. Then count 4 from the RIGHT in the answer, adding zeros on the left if needed. 615 with 4 places = 0.0615."},{id:"fc09",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What happens when you multiply a decimal by 10, 100, and 1000? Give examples.",back:"Multiplying by 10: shift decimal point 1 place to the RIGHT. Example: 3.456 \xd7 10 = 34.56. Multiplying by 100: shift 2 places right. 3.456 \xd7 100 = 345.6. Multiplying by 1000: shift 3 places right. 3.456 \xd7 1000 = 3456. Rule: Count zeros in the power of 10 = number of places to shift right.",memory_hook:"\xd710 = shift RIGHT 1. \xd7100 = right 2. \xd71000 = right 3. 'Multiply = grow = move right on number line.' Count zeros \u2192 count shifts. 3.456 \xd7 1000: shift 3 right \u2192 3456."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What happens when you divide a decimal by 10, 100, and 1000? Give examples.",back:"Dividing by 10: shift decimal point 1 place to the LEFT. Example: 47.3 \xf7 10 = 4.73. Dividing by 100: shift 2 places left. 47.3 \xf7 100 = 0.473. Dividing by 1000: shift 3 places left. 47.3 \xf7 1000 = 0.0473. Rule: Count zeros in the power of 10 = number of places to shift left.",memory_hook:"\xf710 = shift LEFT 1. \xf7100 = left 2. \xf71000 = left 3. 'Division = shrink = move left on number line.' LEFT = less. Add zeros to the left if needed (0.0473 has a leading zero after the decimal)."},{id:"fc11",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Find: 0.4 \xd7 0.04 \xd7 10",back:"Step 1: 0.4 \xd7 0.04. Digits: 4 \xd7 4 = 16. Decimal places: 1+2=3. Result: 0.016. Step 2: 0.016 \xd7 10 = 0.16 (shift decimal right 1). Answer: 0.16.",memory_hook:"Multi-step decimal: do one operation at a time. 0.4 \xd7 0.04 first (count 3 decimal places \u2192 0.016), then \xd7 10 (shift right 1 \u2192 0.16). Never rush \u2014 one step at a time is error-free."},{id:"fc12",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How do you divide a decimal by a decimal? Find: 2.7 \xf7 0.03",back:"To divide decimals: multiply both numbers by a power of 10 to make the divisor a whole number. 0.03 has 2 decimal places \u2192 multiply both by 100: 2.7 \xd7 100 = 270. 0.03 \xd7 100 = 3. Now divide: 270 \xf7 3 = 90. Answer: 2.7 \xf7 0.03 = 90. The ratio stays the same when both are multiplied by the same number.",memory_hook:"Divide decimals: REMOVE the decimal from the bottom by multiplying both top and bottom by 10/100. Like rationalising a fraction \u2014 make the denominator 'whole' first."},{id:"fc13",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How do you convert a fraction to a decimal? Convert 3/8 and 5/6 to decimals.",back:"To convert fraction to decimal: DIVIDE numerator by denominator. 3/8 = 3 \xf7 8 = 0.375 (terminating decimal). 5/6 = 5 \xf7 6 = 0.8333\u2026 = 0.8\u03043\u0304 (repeating/recurring decimal). Terminating decimals: the division ends. Recurring decimals: a digit or group repeats forever. Both are valid decimal representations of fractions.",memory_hook:"Fraction \u2192 Decimal: DIVIDE top by bottom. 3/8 = 3\xf78 = 0.375. Easy fractions to memorise: 1/4=0.25, 1/2=0.5, 3/4=0.75, 1/5=0.2, 1/8=0.125. These appear in real life constantly."},{id:"fc14",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A car's fuel efficiency is 15.6 km/litre. How far can it go on 12.5 litres?",back:"Distance = efficiency \xd7 fuel = 15.6 \xd7 12.5. Step 1: 156 \xd7 125 = 19,500. Step 2: Decimal places: 1+1=2. Step 3: 19,500 \u2192 195.00 km. Answer: The car can travel 195 km on 12.5 litres.",memory_hook:"Distance = rate \xd7 quantity. Always identify what's being multiplied. Decimal multiplication: ignore point \u2192 multiply digits \u2192 count and insert decimal places in answer."},{id:"fc15",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the product of a fraction and its reciprocal? Why?",back:"The product of any non-zero fraction and its reciprocal is always 1. (a/b) \xd7 (b/a) = ab/ba = 1. Examples: (3/7) \xd7 (7/3) = 21/21 = 1. (5) \xd7 (1/5) = 5/5 = 1. This is why dividing by a fraction equals multiplying by its reciprocal \u2014 the divisor multiplied by its reciprocal gives 1 (it cancels itself). Reciprocal of 0 does not exist (0 \xd7 anything = 0, never 1).",memory_hook:"Fraction \xd7 its reciprocal = 1. They are each other's 'inverse'. Like +5 and \u22125 (additive inverses that sum to 0), 3/7 and 7/3 are multiplicative inverses that multiply to 1."},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"\u2157 of the 450 students in a school are girls. How many girls are there?",back:"Girls = \u2157 of 450 = \u2157 \xd7 450 = (3 \xd7 450)/5 = 1350/5 = 270. Answer: 270 girls. Check: Boys = 450 \u2212 270 = 180. Girls/Boys = 270/180 = 3/2, so Girls fraction = 3/(3+2) = 3/5 \u2713.",memory_hook:"'Of' = multiply. \u2157 of 450 = \u2157 \xd7 450. Simplify before multiplying: 450/5 = 90, then \xd7 3 = 270. Always check: does the answer make sense? 270 out of 450 is just over half \u2014 and 3/5 > 1/2, so yes it's correct."},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Cloth costs \u20b985.50 per metre. What is the cost of 6.5 metres?",back:"Cost = 85.50 \xd7 6.5. Step 1: Ignore decimals. 8550 \xd7 65 = 555,750. Step 2: Decimal places: 2+1=3. Step 3: 555750 \u2192 555.750 = \u20b9555.75. Answer: \u20b9555.75 for 6.5 metres.",memory_hook:"Shopping decimal problems: cost per unit \xd7 quantity = total cost. Always: multiply the digits, then count the decimal places. \u20b9555.75 \u2014 two decimal places for paise (2+1=3 places total, and money answers have 2 decimal places \u2014 the answer worked out correctly here)."},{id:"fc18",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a terminating decimal and a non-terminating recurring decimal? Give one example of each.",back:"Terminating decimal: the decimal representation ends (terminates) after a finite number of digits. Example: 3/4 = 0.75 (ends after 2 digits). Fractions with denominators that have only 2 and 5 as prime factors give terminating decimals. Non-terminating recurring decimal: the decimal goes on forever with a repeating pattern. Example: 1/3 = 0.333\u2026 = 0.3\u0304. 2/7 = 0.285714285714\u2026 These are written with a bar over the repeating part.",memory_hook:"Terminating = TERMINATES (ends). 3/4 = 0.75 \u2014 clean and finished. Recurring = RECURS (repeats). 1/3 = 0.333... never ends. Denominator rule: factors of 2 and 5 only \u2192 terminating. Any other prime factor \u2192 recurring."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Evaluate: (3/4 \xf7 9/16) + (5/6 \xd7 2/5)",back:"Part 1: (3/4) \xf7 (9/16) = (3/4) \xd7 (16/9) = 48/36 = 4/3. Part 2: (5/6) \xd7 (2/5) = 10/30 = 1/3. Total: 4/3 + 1/3 = 5/3 = 1\u2154. Answer: 1\u2154.",memory_hook:"Multi-step fractions: solve each operation (\xf7 or \xd7) separately first, then add or subtract. Follow BODMAS \u2014 here both are equal priority, so left to right. Then add results with a common denominator."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Find: 34.6 \xf7 100 and 0.057 \xd7 1000",back:"34.6 \xf7 100: shift decimal LEFT 2 places \u2192 0.346. Answer: 0.346. 0.057 \xd7 1000: shift decimal RIGHT 3 places \u2192 57.0 = 57. Answer: 57. Both are quick 'shift the point' operations \u2014 no actual multiplication or division needed.",memory_hook:"Power of 10 shortcuts: \xf7100 = left 2, \xd71000 = right 3. INSTANT answers \u2014 no long multiplication needed. These appear in unit conversions (cm to m \xf7100, km to m \xd71000) every day in science and real life."}]}},2955,[]);